Subject: syscall: add sockets over WASIX on wasip1 behind the wasix build tag
Last-Update: 2026-10-04

WASI preview 1 has no socket calls beyond accepting on, reading, writing
and shutting down an inherited socket. WASIX, the extension a WASIX
runtime provides in the wasix_32v1 import namespace beside
wasi_snapshot_preview1, has the rest: sock_open, sock_bind, sock_listen,
an accept that reports the peer, sock_connect, the local and peer
addresses, options, and datagram send and receive.

With the wasix build tag Socket, Bind, Listen, Accept, Connect,
Getsockname, Getpeername, SetsockoptInt, GetsockoptInt, Recvfrom and
Sendto are implemented over those calls; without it the ENOSYS stubs
remain, now in a file of their own. Addresses are converted to WASIX's
addr_port layout; families, protocols and options to its numbering. A
non-blocking Connect reports EINPROGRESS until the socket becomes
writable, when GetsockoptInt with SO_ERROR answers from the runtime's
LastError option. A Sendto with no address sends to a connected socket's
peer. A runtime may report a port with its bytes swapped from the order
it was given in, so, as wasix-libc does, the package binds a throwaway
socket to a known port once to learn whether ports read back swapped.
The option names take Linux's numbers, and so do the two the fake
network defines, on every wasip1 build, so that none collide.

